{
  "image_name": "00edcc37e7ae2cdabdabdbb7b75ff98d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how the TARDBP gene, which encodes TDP43, is processed under normal conditions and how it regulates itself when TDP43 is abundant. On the left, steady-state RNA splicing generates several mature TARDBP mRNA isoforms through combinations of alternative introns and alternative polyadenylation sites. These transcripts retain the coding region and are exported from the nucleus to the cytoplasm, where ribosomes translate them into TDP43 protein. The main point is that normal splicing and polyadenylation produce productive mRNAs.\n\nOn the right, excess TDP43 feeds back onto its own pre-mRNA by binding within the 3′ region labeled TDPBR. This promotes alternative splicing outcomes that make the RNA nonproductive. One route introduces an exon-exon junction downstream of a premature stop codon, so after export and translation the transcript is targeted by nonsense-mediated decay, shown with factors such as EJC and SURF. A second route produces an alternatively processed RNA that appears to be retained in the nucleus and then degraded by the exosome. Overall, the figure depicts autoregulation of TDP43 levels by shifting TARDBP RNA processing away from productive mRNAs and toward decay pathways.",
